WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:10.150
One thing we must do as a nation is welcome
our returning citizens back to the community.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.910
But we need to make sure that when they come
back that they’re ready to do so.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:19.300
You know, nationally that over 2/3 of those
who leave prisons actually return within three

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.300
years?

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.450
That’s a big challenge.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.440
One way to address that is to invest in them
before they leave.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.770
I had a chance to visit the Prison Entrepreneurship
Program in Texas where the recidivism rate

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.180
is only seven percent.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.330
And they do four things.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:36.430
Number one, there’s a course focused on
character development.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.210
Number two, there’s a course focused on
entrepreneurship.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.190
Number three, there’s a business plan competition.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:46.890
And number four, once you leave there are
support services they provide.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.710
I think the program is a step in the right
direction.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.800
Once you teach someone who’s returning home
how to take care of themselves, they can take

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.690
care of themselves, their family, and ultimately
the nation.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.320
By investing in people and investing in human
dignity, we invest in parents, we invest in

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.090
human assets, but more importantly, we make
a safe and sound nation of ourselves.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:10.869
So what do you think can be done to help returning
citizens from prison?

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:15.750
Also tell us what ideas AEI scholars should
focus on in 60 seconds.

